2. Practical One: Windows Device Compliance Policy

Objective

Create a compliance policy in Microsoft Intune to establish minimum security requirements for a managed Windows device.

Policy Configuration

Policy name: WIN11-Lab-Compliance-Baseline

Platform: Windows 10 and later.

Description: Baseline compliance requirements for the Windows 11 Intune lab device.

Configuration performed

The compliance policy included the following settings:

Setting   Configuration

Minimum OS version > 10.0.22621.0

Firewall >  Required

Antivirus > Required

Antispyware > Required

Microsoft Defender Antimalware > Required

Microsoft Defender security intelligence > Required to be up to date

Real-time protection > Required

The policy was assigned to All Devices.

The configured noncompliance action was to mark a device noncompliant immediately if it failed to meet the applicable requirements.

Results

The Intune compliance monitoring page displayed:

* Compliant devices: 1
* Noncompliant devices: 0
* Other devices: 0
* Total devices: 1

This indicated that the device was reported as compliant at the time of observation.

Important distinction: A compliant status confirms that Intune reported the device as meeting the configured compliance requirements. It does not prove that every possible security control on the device is effective.

3. Practical Two: Windows Defender Firewall Configuration

Objective

Create a device configuration profile to manage Windows Defender Firewall settings through Microsoft Intune.

Policy details

Profile name: WIN11-LAB-Windows-FIREWALL

Platform: Windows.

Description: Configure Windows Defender Firewall settings to protect the Windows 11 lab device from unauthorized network connections.

Verification

The Intune policy summary displayed the following results: 
Status  Devices

Succeeded >1

Error >0

Conflict >0

Not applicable >0

In progress >0

This demonstrated that Intune reported a successful policy application for one device.

On the Windows 11 virtual machine, Windows PowerShell was used to inspect the firewall profiles.

Command executed: Get-NetFirewallProfile |
Format-List Name, Enabled, DefaultInboundAction, DefaultOutboundAction

The output showed:
Firewall profile | Enabled | Inbound action | Outbound action
Domain.             True.       Block.         Allow
Private.            True.      Block.           Allow
Public.             True.       Block.          Allow


Interpretation

* Domain: Applies when Windows identifies a network as a domain network.
* Private: Applies to networks classified as private.
* Public: Applies to networks classified as public.
* Enabled = True: The firewall is enabled for the profile.
* Inbound = Block: Unsolicited inbound connections are blocked by default, subject to applicable rules and exceptions.
* Outbound = Allow: Outbound connections are allowed by default unless a rule or other applicable configuration blocks them.

The PowerShell output showed that all three firewall profiles were enabled, with inbound traffic blocked by default and outbound traffic allowed by default.

Important distinction: The PowerShell output shows the firewall’s effective profile settings at the time of inspection. It does not, by itself, prove that every setting came from Intune or that the profile will prevent every unauthorized connection.

