5. Key Lessons Learned

Compliance policies and configuration profiles serve different purposes

A compliance policy evaluates whether a device meets defined requirements.

A configuration profile delivers supported configuration settings to a device.

For example, a compliance policy can require the firewall to be enabled, while a configuration profile can configure supported firewall settings.

Policy deployment must be verified

Creating a policy is not the end of the process.

An administrator should examine Intune’s deployment reports and, where appropriate, verify the resulting settings on the managed device.

Device compliance is not the same as complete security

A device reported as compliant can still have security weaknesses that are not covered by the configured compliance policy.

Compliance policies should therefore be combined with appropriate security configurations, monitoring, updates, and other endpoint protection measures.

